      "Step 1: Understanding the Concept:\nA grammar is ambiguous if there is at least one string that can be generated using two or more\ndistinct parse trees (or leftmost derivations).\nStep 2: Detailed Explanation:\nLet's try to generate the string \"b\": 1.S\u2192bS\u2192b\u03f5=b2.S\u2192aSbS. If we let the\nfirstS\u2192\u03f5and the secondS\u2192bS\u2192b\u03f5, we geta\u03f5b(b\u03f5) =ab. Let's try string \"ab\":\n1.S\u2192aSbS\u2192a(\u03f5)b(\u03f5) =ab2.S\u2192bS . . .(doesn't start with a). Wait, look at the\nruleS\u2192aSbS|bS|\u03f5. To prove ambiguity, we need one string with two trees. Con-\nsider string \"b\": Tree 1:S\u2192bS\u2192b(\u03f5) Is there another? No. Consider \"abab\": Tree 1:\nS\u2192aSbSwhere firstS\u2192\u03f5and secondS\u2192aSbS . . .If a grammar has rules likeS\u2192SSor\nmultiple recursive paths to the same prefix, it is often ambiguous. In this case, the trailingS\nin both productions often allows for multiple ways to \"end\" a string using\u03f5.\nStep 3: Final Answer:\nThe grammar is ambiguous (specifically due to the recursiveSat the end of both production\nchoices).",
      "Let's try to generate the string \"b\": 1.S\u2192bS\u2192b\u03f5=b2.S\u2192aSbS. If we let the\nfirstS\u2192\u03f5and the secondS\u2192bS\u2192b\u03f5, we geta\u03f5b(b\u03f5) =ab. Let's try string \"ab\":\n1.S\u2192aSbS\u2192a(\u03f5)b(\u03f5) =ab2.S\u2192bS . . .(doesn't start with a). Wait, look at the\nruleS\u2192aSbS|bS|\u03f5. To prove ambiguity, we need one string with two trees. Con-\nsider string \"b\": Tree 1:S\u2192bS\u2192b(\u03f5) Is there another? No. Consider \"abab\": Tree 1:\nS\u2192aSbSwhere firstS\u2192\u03f5and secondS\u2192aSbS . . .If a grammar has rules likeS\u2192SSor\nmultiple recursive paths to the same prefix, it is often ambiguous. In this case, the trailingS\nin both productions often allows for multiple ways to \"end\" a string using\u03f5.",
