      "[Q43 \u00b7 Technical \u00b7 2 Marks] [GATE 2026 CS2] If an IP network uses a subnet mask of 255.255.240.0, the maximum number\nof IP addresses that can be assigned to network interface is .\nSolution:\nStep 1: Understanding the Concept:\nAssignably IP addresses are calculated based on the number of bits reserved for the host. We\nsubtract 2 from the total combinations to account for the Network ID and Broadcast address.\n37\nStep 2: Key Formula or Approach:\nNumber of usable hosts = 2 (32-CIDR) -2\nStep 3: Detailed Explanation:\nSubnet Mask: 255.255.240.0 - 1st octet: 255 (8 bits) - 2nd octet: 255 (8 bits) - 3rd octet: 240\nin binary is 11110000 (4 bits) - 4th octet: 0 (0 bits) Total Network bits (1s) = 8 + 8 + 4 = 20.\nTotal Host bits (0s) = 32-20 = 12. Number of IP addresses = 2 12 -2 = 4096-2 = 4094.\nStep 4: Final Answer:\nThe maximum number of IP addresses is 4094.\nQuick Tip\nMemorizing powers of 2 is essential for networking. 2 10 = 1024, so 212 is just 1024\u00d74 =\n4096.",
